2.1a this situation is called thread interleaving and it's happens because of context switching
what happened is that after the first line(runner 4 ran 100 meters) the thread's of runner 4 got his cpu powers
away and then given to runner 3 which ran 1 more meter and then again JVM did a context switch
and gave cpu time to runner's 4 thread which allowed him to output his place 

2.1b yes such situation can accure indeed, and it can happen when runner 4 got to 100 meters which 
will mean he finished and before he will managed to tell the track that he finished and what's his place
his cpu time will be taken away and be given to runner's 3 thread which will allow him to finish the race and tell the system that he finished before
runner 4 managed to tell so according to the jvm runner 3 came first when in reality it was runner 4
and it can be even worse when runner 4 finished but then cpu was given to runner 3 and finished but then again given to runner 2 and he finished and 
also manged to report he finished so he will be proclamied first even though he came third

2.2 no, higher prioroity doesnt gurantree higher speed 
all it means is that it will try to give it more cpu time 
but keyword is try and not will

3.a there's inheretance from thread
    and Runnable

    let's starts with thread, we make out class as a child of the class thread so it will look something like
    class racer exteands thread, which means we are a child of a class thread and then we implemant in our code run()
    and just use racer().start new()
    pros: easy to use and setup
    cons: because it make us as a child of the class thread it will block us from resuing the class again through inheretance
    which make it less reusable and less flexable


    Runnable we make a class as we would always do but we use implemant which will make it look like
    class racer implements runnable, and then we also add a run() method 
    and in our main we declare our class like we would always do and then to make it run on a thread we will do
    Thread t1 = new Thread(racer)

    pros: more flexable as it still allows us to use inheretance and to reuse the class 
    also easy separation so we can choose who runs the thread and make it to runs on few different threads at the same time

    cons: more overhead as it require us to create a thread object to run it


    3.b When a class implements Runnable, it does not represent a thread.
It only represents the code that should run only the Thread class can create a new thread
call start() therefore, to run a Runnable in a new thread, we must create a Runnable object
pass it to a Thread object call start() on the Thread